"A request-reply diagram connects requests and responses across explicit channels using correlation and timeout rules.\n\nUse when: Explain asynchronous work whose caller still needs a result. Review reply routing, correlation, and late responses.\n\nAvoid when: The sender announces facts without expecting a response.\n\nVisual grammar: Participants denote requester and replier roles. Separate arrows or channels carry requests and replies. Correlation IDs, reply destinations, and timeout markers connect the two directions.\n\nBuild: Choose one operation and its expected response contract. Draw request and reply channels with distinct directions. Add correlation, timeout, and duplicate-response handling. Place response labels on a separate route so opposing arrows cannot overlap.\n\nCheck: Each reply can be matched to an outstanding request. A timeout does not imply the remote operation was canceled. Retries identify whether operation identity is reused. Request and response text remain clear of participant borders.\n\nPitfalls: Using two arrows without explaining correlation. Assuming an asynchronous transport eliminates caller waiting or uncertainty.\n\nExample: A report requester sends a job with correlation r17; the worker replies to the designated channel with the same identifier.\n\nAlternatives: Sequence diagram, Scatter-gather diagram."
